  "prompt_spec": {
    "original_prompt": "The role of phantasia and Erschlossenheit in disclosing the environment:\nhow Aristotle's account of phantasia in De Anima III.3 anticipates\nHeidegger's concept of disclosedness (Erschlossenheit) in Being and Time.\n\nSpecifically address:\n(a) How Aristotle's phantasia functions as the faculty that discloses appearances (phainomena),\n(b) How Heidegger's Erschlossenheit structures the disclosure of Being-in-the-world,\n(c) The structural parallel between these two modes of disclosure,\n(d) Where the two frameworks diverge — Aristotle's faculty psychology vs. Heidegger's existential analytic.\n\nInclude verbatim quotations from both De Anima and Being and Time.\nTarget length: 1000-1500 words. Write as a single coherent section suitable for a doctoral dissertation.",
    "research_questions": [
      "How does Aristotle's account of phantasia in De Anima III.3 anticipate Heidegger's concept of disclosedness (Erschlossenheit) in Being and Time?",
      "How does Aristotle's phantasia function as the faculty that discloses appearances (phainomena)?",
      "How does Heidegger's Erschlossenheit structure the disclosure of Being-in-the-world?",
      "What are the structural parallels between Aristotle's phantasia and Heidegger's Erschlossenheit as modes of disclosure?",
      "Where do Aristotle's faculty psychology and Heidegger's existential analytic diverge in their accounts of disclosure?"
    ],
    "required_facets": [
      {
        "facet_id": "cf117ecc-4376-4bbe-b461-c9d3c89b56b9",
        "name": "Aristotle's Phantasia as Disclosure of Phainomena",
        "description": "How does Aristotle theorize phantasia in De Anima Book III Chapter 3 as the psychological faculty that mediates between sensation (aisthesis) and thought (noesis), enabling the disclosure of appearances (phainomena) to the soul? This facet examines Aristotle's definition of phantasia as distinct from perception and belief, its role in making absent objects present to consciousness, and its function as the intermediary capacity that allows beings to appear as they are. Key passages include Aristotle's claim that phantasia arises from actual sensation and his analysis of how phantasia enables thinking about objects not immediately present to the senses, thus constituting a primary mode of world-disclosure in Aristotelian psychology.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "4108f226-fc41-4353-af8a-214521cc7a25",
        "name": "Heidegger's Erschlossenheit and Being-in-the-World",
        "description": "How does Heidegger articulate Erschlossenheit (disclosedness) in Being and Time as the fundamental existential structure through which Dasein's Being-in-the-world is opened up and made intelligible? This facet investigates Heidegger's analysis of disclosedness as comprising Befindlichkeit (attunement), Verstehen (understanding), and Rede (discourse), examining how these existential structures constitute the clearing (Lichtung) in which entities can show themselves. Focus on Heidegger's distinction between ontic disclosure of particular entities and ontological disclosedness as Dasein's basic way of being, particularly in sections 28-34 and 44 of Being and Time, where Heidegger explicates how world is always already disclosed prior to any theoretical apprehension.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "573fa958-73cd-405f-b273-1baf49399a66",
        "name": "Structural Parallels: Mediation and Pre-theoretical Access",
        "description": "What are the structural and phenomenological parallels between Aristotle's phantasia and Heidegger's Erschlossenheit as modes of disclosure that operate prior to or alongside explicit theoretical cognition? This facet examines how both concepts function as mediating structures that enable access to beings: phantasia mediates between sensation and intellection in Aristotle's faculty psychology, while Erschlossenheit constitutes the pre-theoretical openness that makes any encounter with entities possible in Heidegger's existential analytic. Investigate scholarly interpretations that trace Heidegger's appropriation of Aristotelian themes, particularly how both thinkers reject purely representational models of cognition in favor of accounts that emphasize the primacy of disclosure, appearance, and the showing-itself of phenomena.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "8a76e253-0a65-4809-b0ab-a27aa61da96d",
        "name": "Divergence: Faculty Psychology vs. Existential Analytic",
        "description": "Where and how do Aristotle's faculty-psychological framework and Heidegger's existential analytic fundamentally diverge in their accounts of disclosure, particularly regarding the ontological status of the disclosing subject and the metaphysical commitments underlying each approach? This facet examines the critical differences: Aristotle's phantasia operates within a substance-ontology where the soul (psyche) is understood as the form of a living body with distinct faculties or powers (dynameis), while Heidegger's Erschlossenheit belongs to an existential-ontological analysis that rejects substance metaphysics in favor of understanding Dasein as Being-in-the-world. Investigate how Aristotle's hylomorphic framework, his commitment to faculty psychology, and his teleological naturalism contrast with Heidegger's anti-Cartesian, anti-substantialist phenomenology and his critique of the subject-object dichotomy.",
        "facet_role": "core",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      },
      {
        "facet_id": "51c5f97b-b916-4b36-bd29-6e3869383811",
        "name": "Scholarly Reception and Interpretive Debates",
        "description": "How have contemporary scholars in phenomenology, ancient philosophy, and Heidegger studies interpreted the relationship between Aristotelian phantasia and Heideggerian Erschlossenheit, and what are the major interpretive debates regarding whether Heidegger's concept represents a genuine appropriation, transformation, or misreading of Aristotle? This facet surveys secondary literature examining Heidegger's early lectures on Aristotle, his engagement with De Anima in his 1920s courses, and scholarly assessments of continuity versus rupture between these frameworks. Include perspectives from scholars who emphasize Heidegger's debt to Aristotelian phenomenology as well as critics who argue that Heidegger's existential analytic fundamentally distorts or departs from Aristotle's naturalistic psychology.",
        "facet_role": "supporting",
        "evidence_policy_for_kind": {},
        "archived": false,
        "atoms_mode": "analytics",
        "reverse_check_mode": "warn"
      }
